A||||12||ChopTop||Engine wants to stall when stopping, brakes fade||||1||
Z||000000||ChopTop||10-18-2001||08:34 AM||||Hi Folks<BR>I could use some help troubleshooting.  My wife's 88 4cy, automatic, 119,000 miles.  Basically what it wants to do is stall when you come to stop, the engine light comes on and the brakes fade.<BR>We've already taken it to our mechanic who looked it over and replaced sparkplugs and checked the wires and the timing, 80 bucks, and a new battery later, and the car still has the same problem.<BR>I thought it could possible be the TCC so I unplugged that last night and drove it around, the engine light came on shortly afterward, and there was practically no braking power while the engine light was on.  Once the light went out, the brakes returned to normal, and I continued to drive around for several more minutes, and it ran fine.<BR>This morning I drove it to work, the engine light came on, and again the car felt like it was going to stall out while I was stopped.  I did notice the RPMs dropped down to around 600 RPMs when idling.<BR>I do not own any diagnostic equipment to retrieve codes and the cigarette lighter is screwed up so I don't think I can use the paper clip method to retrieve codes.<BR>Could I have a vacuum leak, bad sensors?  Suggestions would be helpful.  This has been a good car for us and we do plan on keeping it for awhile.<BR>Thanks very much.||||reg||1
Z||000001||SKIDMARK||10-18-2001||08:54 AM||||There is a check valve for the brake booster just in front of the engine on the firewall. I've seen a couple of those rust out and leak causing similar problems to what you describe. You can still get the trouble codes. The lighter doesn't have anything to do with the connector. Take the plastic cover surrounding the lighter off (2 t-10 Torx screws) and get a repair manual that describes the codes and how to get them from your system. Be careful because the connector is described backwards in my manual (Haynes). Good luck and let us know what you find.<P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||1
Z||000002||V8Dreamer||10-18-2001||09:32 AM||||Just a shot in the dark here, but maybe the torque converter is staying locked when it shouldn't???  In my old Grand Am it liked to spit and sputter at stops due to this problem.  Again, I doubt this is your problem, but it's worth a shot.<P>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000003||ray b||10-18-2001||12:26 PM||||VACUME LEAK FIX IT BEFORE DRIVING||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||10-18-2001||01:12 PM||||Don't vac leaks cause HIGH idle though?<P>But it would definatly explain your lack of braking power...||||reg||1
Z||000005||TooManyFieros||10-18-2001||01:35 PM||||Vac leak and a good one too, if you lose brakes that fast.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChopTop||10-18-2001||06:01 PM||||It ran fine on the way home, but I only live two miles from work so I let it idle in the driveway for about 15 minutes, sounded fine.  Then I put it in neutral and ran it up to 2500RPM, I could hear it missing, I let off on the gas and I thought it was going to stall out, it ran ruff for several moments, then the RPMs picked back up and it would idle fine.  The engine light did come on but would go out once the RPMs pick up.  I'll try and check for vacuum leaks this weekend.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tigger||10-18-2001||07:52 PM||||Check the EGR valve and hoses.<P>The automatic Indy John had, had a similar problem.  Turned out the stem at the base of the EGR valve had rusted through and was competely loose.  Depending on how it sat over the base it would run ok, idle really bad, idle low, and with the car stopped and in drive, it wanted to stall.<P>New EGR fixed the problem and that just might also be the source of the vacuum leak.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||operator64||10-18-2001||09:27 PM||||   I do believe it sounds like the brake booster. If you have a helper pull the 90 degree elbow out of the front of the booster, put thumb over the 90 degree elbow, have your helper start the car. At this point the 90 degree elbow should be sucking on your thumb, now have the helper press on the brake pedal, does it kill or try to stall out the motor as it did before? If not then the brake booster is leaking you will have to replace it. <BR>   Hope this is helpful.<BR> Michael||||reg||1
Z||000009||SLYGUY||10-19-2001||12:00 AM||||SKIDMARK:<BR>The Haynes manual was backwards in its description of grounding out the terminals in that plug behind that cigarette lighter panel. Glad I'm not the only one to notice that!! After about a half an hour of trying to get a good short in the terminals it was telling me to short, I did something I would never do otherwise. I put the paper clip I was using in the exact opposite terminals on the bottom row and then I got the code displays.<P>I agree with the EGR ideas. My car ran like doo doo without a new one. Also check out the distributor cap and rotor. <BR>Bottle of fuel system cleaner is a good idea too. <BR>Brakes... Very important to have this stuff looked at by a pro. Your loved ones lives are on the line with this one.<P>Hope all turns out well!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000010||ChopTop||10-21-2001||10:53 AM||||Thanks everyone, it was the EGR valve.  Picked up a new Delco from NAPA.  Car's running fine again.  Still not sure why it was affecting the brakes though.  I'm going to assume loss of engine power also mean loss of brake power even if the car hasn't completely stall out.  Thanks again.||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||10-21-2001||01:57 PM||||You should also clean or replace the check valve on the brake booster. That valve traps enough reserve capasity in the booster to give 1-2 pedal stomps. If the valve is messed up, and it sound like yours is, then the booster will loose that reserve and go flat the second the engine isn't at full manifold vac.<P>Most ov the common booster check valves are listed in MotorMite's master catalog. They may also be racked with MotorMite's red HELP! stuff in many parts stores.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000012||falconhulk||10-22-2001||08:22 AM||||SLYGUY<BR>Man you are right about that haynes manual!  When I got my car a few months ago I had a problem similar to the one described here except it only happened after it rained.  I went to check the codes and nothing happened.  I tried to start my car after wards and it would crank but not turn.  I could not hear trhe fuel pump coming on so I checked and it was the fuse.  So what I learned?  Those 2 terminals the haynes manual shows will blow the fuel pump fuse if shorted.  There is another pic in that manual that shows it correctly though.||||reg||1
